 Earth Orbit","image":null,"lsp":"United States Air Force","pad":"Space Launch Complex 1E","location":"Vandenberg SFB, CA, USA","lat":"34.756","lon":"-120.6263","pad_wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vandenberg_Space_Launch_Complex_1","pad_map":"https://www.google.com/maps?q=34.756,-120.6263","net_precision":"","webcast":false,"webcast_url":"","hold_reason":"","fail_reason":"","weather":"","type":"detailed","program":[],"orbital_count":63,"year_orbital":9,"window_start":"1960-04-15T20:30:37Z","window_end":"1960-04-15T20:30:37Z","probability":null,"boosters":[]},{"id":"620237e7-82b2-44c9-affb-93744951d457","name":"Vostok-L 8K72 | Luna-4b","net":"1960-04-16T16:07:41Z","status":"Launch Failure","rocket":"Vostok-L 8K72","mission":"Luna-4b","mission_desc":"Luna 1960B was the third Soviet attempt to photograph the far side of the Moon, the first being the highly successful Luna 3 mission and the second being the unsuccessful Luna 1960A. Luna 1960B was launched on April 16, 1960. It was designed to replicate the Luna 3 mission, but with higher resolution cameras and a closer approach to the Moon.","orbit":"Lunar flyby","image":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/vostok-l_2528l_image_20251204203918.jpg","lsp":"Soviet Space Program","pad":"1/5","location":"Baikonur Cosmodrome, Republic of Kazakhstan","lat":"45.92","lon":"63.342","pad_wiki":"","pad_map":"https://www.google.com/maps?q=45.92,63.342","net_precision":"","webcast":false,"webcast_url":"","hold_reason":"","fail_reason":"","weather":"","type":"detailed","program":["Luna"],"orbital_count":64,"year_orbital":10,"window_start":"1960-04-16T16:07:41Z","window_end":"1960-04-16T16:07:41Z","probability":null,"boosters":[]},{"id":"b4158c69-88e9-4bfa-927f-c3489cf48eda","name":"Thor Delta | Echo","net":"1960-05-13T09:16:05Z","status":"Launch Failure","rocket":"Thor Delta","mission":"Echo","mission_desc":"The Echo 1 spacecraft was a 30.48 m diameter balloon of mylar polyester film 0.0127 mm thick. The spacecraft was designed as a passive communications reflector for transcontinental and intercontinental telephone (voice), radio, and television signals. It had 107.9 MHz beacon transmitters for telemetry purposes. These transmitters were powered by five nickel-cadmium batteries that were charged by 70 solar cells mounted on the balloon. Because of the large area-to-mass ratio of the spacecraft, data for the calculation of atmospheric density and solar pressure could be acquired. The spacecraft was also used to evaluate the technical feasibility of satellite triangulation during the latter portion of its life. Echo 1 failed during the coast period after launch, as the attitude control jets on the second stage failed and the spacecraft did not achieve orbit. Echo 1A was a successful relaunch.","orbit":"Elliptical Orbit","image":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/thor-delta_laun_image_20251123094044.jpg","lsp":"United States Air Force","pad":"Space Launch Complex 17A","location":"Cape Canaveral SFS, FL, USA","lat":"28.4472","lon":"-80.565","pad_wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Canaveral_Space_Launch_Complex_17","pad_map":"https://www.google.com/maps?q=28.4472,-80.565","net_precision":"","webcast":false,"webcast_url":"","hold_reason":"","fail_reason":"","weather":"","type":"detailed","program":[],"orbital_count":65,"year_orbital":11,"window_start":"1960-05-13T09:16:05Z","window_end":"1960-05-13T09:16:05Z","probability":null,"boosters":[]},{"id":"02b8bb48-37a1-4f43-9112-33d8e8589a75","name":"Vostok 8K72 | Korabl'-Sputnik-1","net":"1960-05-15T00:00:05Z","status":"Launch Successful","rocket":"Vostok 8K72","mission":"Korabl'-Sputnik-1","mission_desc":"Korabl-Sputnik was the first test flight of the Vostok programme and the first Vostok spacecraft.","orbit":"Low Earth Orbit","image":null,"lsp":"Soviet Space Program","pad":"1/5","location":"Baikonur Cosmodrome, Republic of Kazakhstan","lat":"45.92","lon":"63.342","pad_wiki":""