irst attempt to launch a Mercury spacecraft with the Mercury-Redstone Launch Vehicle. Intended to be an uncrewed sub-orbital spaceflight, it was launched on November 21, 1960 from Cape Canaveral Air Force Station, Florida. The launch failed in abnormal fashion: immediately after the Mercury-Redstone rocket started to move, it shut itself down and settled back on the pad, after which the capsule jettisoned its escape rocket and deployed its recovery parachutes. The failure has been referred to as the \"four-inch flight\", for the approximate distance traveled by the launch vehicle.","orbit":"Suborbital","image":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/redstone_image_20190207032627.jpeg","lsp":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration","pad":"Launch Complex 5","location":"Cape Canaveral SFS, FL, USA","lat":"28.43942","lon":"-80.573301","pad_wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Canaveral_Launch_Complex_5","pad_map":"https://www.google.com/maps?q=28.43942,-80.573301","net_precision":"","webcast":false,"webcast_url":"","hold_reason":"","fail_reason":"","weather":"","type":"detailed","program":["Mercury"],"orbital_count":0,"year_orbital":0,"window_start":"1960-11-21T14:00:00Z","window_end":"1960-11-21T14:00:00Z","probability":null,"boosters":[]},{"id":"7907f62e-09cd-476c-afb5-dfb22445caa9","name":"Thor Delta | Tiros 2","net":"1960-11-23T11:13:03Z","status":"Launch Successful","rocket":"Thor Delta","mission":"Tiros 2","mission_desc":"A spin stabilized meteorological satellite. The second in a series of Television Infrared Observation Satellites.","orbit":"Low Earth Orbit","image":"https://thespacedevs-prod.nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com/media/images/thor-delta_laun_image_20251123094044.jpg","lsp":"United States Air Force","pad":"Space Launch Complex 17A","location":"Cape Canaveral SFS, FL, USA","lat":"28.4472","lon":"-80.565","pad_wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Canaveral_Space_Launch_Complex_17","pad_map":"https://www.google.com